  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1107 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nIncreased moisture across region will improve chances for showers\nand thunderstorms the rest of this week and into the weekend. \nThe persistent area of high pressure will shift east toward the\ngulf coast. This will allow a southerly flow of deeper moisture\nmore typical of a Monsoon pattern into New Mexico for the next \nseveral days. The plume of deeper moisture over Arizona will shift\neastward and fully straddle New Mexico by Friday night. \n\nPrecipitable water values have risen above an inch and will peak \nat nearly 1.5 inches by Friday night. The increased moisture \navailability will support longer lived showers and thunderstorms \ncapable of producing periods of heavy rainfall with a potential \nfor localized flooding. Overall rain amounts will vary, which is \ntypical of convective weather patterns, but some areas will \nexperience heavy rainfall from strong thunderstorms, especially in\nthe mountain areas. As rain chances increases for lowlands, so\nwill the potential for localized flooding, especially in urban\nareas toward the weekend.  \n\nBy late Sunday into early Monday, high pressure aloft will \nrebuild over the region, pushing the Monsoonal moisture back to\nthe west over Arizona. The local area will be under an easterly\nflow pattern that will bring in a drier continental air mass that\nwill cause atmospheric moisture levels to drop and with PW's\nfalling below and inch. Lack of moisture will limit rain chances\nto mainly the mountains the first part of next week.",
